// NEGATIVE TEST -- a macro's expansion must parse where it is invoked (PD0079,
// #85 review round 1).
//
// An expansion is spliced in as TEXT and parsed again: as an expression in
// value position, as statements in statement position. The builtin
// `println!(e)` expands to `print((e)); print("\n")` -- no final `;`, and the
// string literal is itself refused in a user macro body (PD0008) -- so in
// statement position it does not parse. The parser's own error used to come
// out bare, "Unexpected end of file", naming no macro and pointing its caret at
// an offset into the expansion rather than at the invocation.
//
// WHY THIS PROGRAM: the invocation is in a trait's DEFAULT body, which nothing
// reads. Main never expanded one, and ran this program and printed 1. Since
// #85 every body is expanded, so the refusal that a free function always got
// now reaches this body too -- and it is required to say what it refuses: the
// macro, the position, the parser's reason and the expansion, at the
// invocation's span.
struct S {
    n: i64,
}

trait T {
    fn f(s: S) {
        println!(1);
    }
}

fn main() {
    print_int(1);
}
